1889 and 2106. In the specific context of conflict, the omission of access to sexual and reproductive health care for victims of sexual violence is unacceptable and undermines their dignity. Furthermore, it is intolerable that the Council fails to recognize that women and girls who have not chosen to become pregnant should have the right to decide to terminate their pregnancies. France will maintain its position that women must be able to make decisions freely about their own bodies and will continue to promote full access to sexual and reproductive health care in every context.\n\nFinally, while this resolution is a step forward, it is not the end of our journey. We call upon all members to continue the fight against sexual violence in conflict.<|im_end|>", "output_display": "Mr. President, France welcomes the adoption of resolution 2467 (2019), submitted by Germany, which we thank for its initiative. This resolution constitutes a significant milestone in our collective action on women, peace and security, reaffirming the framework established by paragraphs 19 of resolution 2106 (2013) and 10 of resolution 1889 (2009). It strengthens the mandate of the Special Representative of the Secretary-General on Sexual Violence in Conflict, Ms. Pramila Patten, and recognizes a survivor-centred approach.\n\nHowever, we must acknowledge that important concessions were made under pressure from several permanent members. We deplore the removal of the reference to sexual and reproductive health, previously agreed upon in resolutions 1889 and 2106. In the specific context of conflict, the omission of access to sexual and reproductive health care for victims of sexual violence is unacceptable and undermines their dignity. Furthermore, it is intolerable that the Council fails to recognize that women and girls who have not chosen to become pregnant should have the right to decide to terminate their pregnancies. France will maintain its position that women must be able to make decisions freely about their own bodies and will continue to promote full access to sexual and reproductive health care in every context.\n\nFinally, while this resolution is a step forward, it is not the end of our journey. We call upon all members to continue the fight against sexual violence in conflict.", "generated_tokens": 306, "generation_seconds": 7.93390716612339, "at_cap": false}
